            <h2 class="comments-header">2 Comments</h2>
        
        <div class="comments-content">
            <div id="comment-38" class="comment">
                <p><strong>bill maugans, <a href="http://thebaseballchronicle.com/essays/the_mick#comment-38" title="Comment permalink">Jun 17, 2009</a></strong></p>
                <p>I quite agree, whenever Mick came up to bat, the anticipation was enormous. I watched the 60 world series on TV and it was such a donnybrook,and Mickey was right in the middle of things. He was not the graceful outfielder Dimaggio was but his speed and arm were as good as any. I watched his duel with Roger in 61 and still think he'd have broken the record except for that hip abscess. It was a sad day all around when he died.
I'd like to put in a good word for the Stan the Man who I met as a 14 yr old living in the St Louis area he was just entering his decline but he was still "the Man" as gracious a human being as he was as good a player, he's still with us, thank God.</p>

            </div>
        </div>
    
        
        <div class="comments-content">
            <div id="comment-50" class="comment">
                <p><strong>Rick Soisson, <a href="http://thebaseballchronicle.com/essays/the_mick#comment-50" title="Comment permalink">Aug  3, 2009</a></strong></p>
                <p>Both this piece and the first comment are nice tributes.  Growing up in Pittsburgh, Mantle was the ultimate nemesis in the '60 World Series.  One was quite aware of the potential for a home run every time he came up.  Musial, of course, was from the Pittsburgh area.  As an amusement, guess how many lifetime triples he had, then look it up.  For myself...a player I saw a great deal?  Clemente.  As much a threat as Mantle, if not more so, for at least a hit, and easily the best thrower of the 50's and 60's.</p>

            </div>
        </div>
    


    
    


